{
  "abstract": "A paradoxical shortcut Collatz segment has a contracting homogeneous coefficient and a nondecreasing endpoint. Rozier and Terracol proved completeness of the 593 known sequences through length 301,993. Combining published shortcut peak and strict ordinary delay records with finite trajectory censuses, we extend their completeness interval to 10,439,860,590 shortcut steps at all starting heights. Reciprocal bounds and a determinant-one lattice reduce the remaining checks to two physical clocks. A census of 594,130,331,777 odd seeds retains 5,001 high hits and excludes the second clock by examining their possible predecessors. At the first unresolved length 10,439,860,591, two-sided source windows and paired scans imply that a strict increase must have least odd departure at least 105,211,889,663. The 38 new source intervals contain 417,485,663,029 starts. We also prove an exact natural density formula for successful lengths, including when nontrivial cycles coexist with unbounded orbits, and give the corresponding prime-date criterion. Density zero is equivalent to universal convergence. An independent single-length implication turns eventual convergence of all strict increases at a specified grade into convergence throughout an explicit interval of original sources. A portable supplement checks the arithmetic, source windows and representative native shards and supplies a checkpointed exhaustive replay. The finite exclusions assume completeness of the cited record tables.",
